---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "/";
---
<Layout title="MIT Inspire Competition — Rahul Jain">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href={base + "origins/"}>&larr; Origins</a>
    <h1 class="page-title"><a href="https://getinspired.mit.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT Inspire Competition<Icon name="external" /></a></h1>
    <p class="page-lede">Two submissions, 2017 and 2018 — alternate finalist / semi-finalist standing</p>
    <section>
      <p class="blurb">
        My research mapped a specific gap in U.S. teen financial education, and made a slightly
        contrarian bet: that the same excitement teenagers had around cryptocurrency in the late 2010s
        could be redirected into actually teaching them how money works, rather than just being a
        speculative curiosity. That research earned alternate finalist and semi-finalist standing in the
        MIT Inspire competition.
      </p>
      <p class="blurb">
        It also turned out to be the seed for Don't Be Broke — the research question came first, the
        venture came after. A year earlier, in the 2017 cycle, I'd submitted a companion piece on the
        classroom side of the same underlying question — whether the technology being pushed on students
        was actually helping them learn.
      </p>

      <div class="abstract-quote">
        <span class="abstract-label">Full Abstract — "Seizing the Emerging Bitcoin Opportunity: Enhancing Financial Literacy in Teenagers" (MIT INSPIRE 2018, researched Oct–Dec 2017)</span>
        <p>
          Bitcoin was more than just a sensation; it has quickly pervaded the market and investment
          strategies around the world and has even become a modern method of payment. Excitement around
          Bitcoin has also quickly revealed the gross lack of financial understanding among students. There
          is a critical need for understanding personal finance and the markets while young, and Bitcoin
          highlights the critical need to utilize this emerging excitement to enhance the culture of
          financial literacy and education in teens around the world.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bitcoin is a digital currency — more generally called cryptocurrency — that has recently gained
          popularity globally and has taken hold in varying degrees across countries. This report
          investigates the top 10 countries deemed "Bitcoin-friendly": Estonia, the United States,
          Denmark, Sweden, South Korea, the Netherlands, Finland, Canada, the United Kingdom, and
          Australia. The top two indicators for Bitcoin acceptance were GDP per capita and financial
          literacy — 8 of the 10 Bitcoin-friendly countries had the highest GDP per capita in the world,
          and 9 of the 10 had the highest financial literacy in the world.
        </p>
        <p>
          Financial literacy among teenagers specifically was found to be an important indicator for what
          Bitcoin and cryptocurrency acceptance will look like in the future. PISA scores were very high
          for Canada, the Netherlands, and Australia, but not for the rest of the list (including the
          United States), which scored below the mean international PISA score — suggesting that while
          cryptocurrency acceptance is high at the moment, it's likely to diminish going forward unless
          financial education for teenagers improves. That improvement wouldn't just help American
          markets; it would help students make responsible decisions without incurring significant
          losses.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="abstract-quote">
        <span class="abstract-label">Full Abstract — "Technology in Education: Understanding the Perspectives and Analyzing the Effects of Computer Integration in High School Learning Environments" (MIT INSPIRE 2017, researched Oct–Dec 2016)</span>
        <p>
          Technology has long influenced the outcomes of education, creating better tools for classroom
          instruction, allowing for better dissemination of information, providing efficient methods of
          testing and instant feedback for students, and offering routes for additional educational
          support. Such tools were historically only implemented in the classroom through teacher use or
          student use outside of the classroom; it was not until recently that <strong>students were provided the
          ability to access these resources themselves</strong> through the use of laptops in the classroom.
        </p>
        <p>
          Colloquially termed EdTech, educational technology has been publicized as having important
          advantages for student education, but when applied practically shows many unreported drawbacks.
          The argument for ubiquitous use of technology in the classroom stems from the ability to
          <strong>equalize access to online resources</strong> and allow a fast, easy way to learn material historically
          presented in large textbooks — one central location for all documents to help with organization,
          and giving all students the same device to help with uniformity, socially and within the
          classroom.
        </p>
        <p>
          This study drew on data and testimony from all stakeholders in the issue — students, parents,
          administrators, and teachers — to formulate a more objective, well-informed opinion on the
          growing EdTech trend and propose a solution for inequities and inefficiencies in existing
          programs. Teachers showed a clear preference for technology given the extra resources it
          provides and the ease of receiving and grading assignments; administrators considered it a
          worthy investment. Students, however, were more apprehensive, citing differences in learning
          styles — <strong>technology didn't appear to be improving test scores</strong> the way it was theorized to, and
          in some cases students felt it was decreasing test scores. Parents were similarly split, some
          worried about distraction and others confident it better reflected the work students would be
          doing in college.
        </p>
        <p>
          Given that split consensus among the most important stakeholder group, the natural solution was
          to <strong>change the requirement of laptop use to an optional choice</strong> in the classroom — a more
          realistic reflection of college life, and a courtesy already extended to teachers, who could
          already choose between instruction online or on paper.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
